      analysis: 'The soliloquy is the most reliable window into a character\'s true psychology in Shakespearean drama — and what it reveals here is Macbeth\'s acute awareness that his desires are sinful. The imperative "hide" is directed at the stars themselves, a blasphemous act of commanding God\'s creation. "Black and deep" is carefully chosen: "black" signals moral corruption and evil, while "deep" suggests these desires are not surface impulses but something fundamental to his identity. The light/dark binary operates throughout the play as a moral framework — light represents God, legitimacy, and natural order; darkness represents sin and chaos. By asking stars to extinguish themselves, Macbeth is asking the divine to look away while he acts against it. The hamartia is explicitly named — ambition — but the fact that he immediately wants to conceal it demonstrates he still possesses a functioning moral conscience.',
      context: 'In Christian theology, which structured Jacobean moral thinking entirely, to sin was one matter — but to deliberately arrange for divine oversight to be removed was an act of profound hubris and blasphemy. The Great Chain of Being, the cosmic hierarchy running from God through monarchs to peasants, was considered sacred and inviolable. Macbeth\'s request that "light" not witness his desires is, in this context, a request for God to be blinded — which would have horrified a Jacobean audience even as they understood the psychological realism of his turmoil.',
