      analysis: 'The simile — flower versus serpent — compresses the play\'s entire theme of appearance versus reality into a single image. "Innocent flower" invokes purity, beauty, and harmlessness; "serpent under\'t" invokes predatory concealment and biblical evil. The biblical allusion to the Garden of Eden is structurally precise: the serpent tempted Eve to transgress divine law, and Lady Macbeth is now tempting Macbeth to do the same. "Under\'t" — the shortened form creates an intimacy, as if the serpent is not merely beneath but fused with the flower. The imperative "look" demonstrates her dominance over Macbeth at this point: she is issuing instructions, and he is receiving them. She defines the terms of the deception they will share, positioning herself as the architect of the crime and him as the performer.',
