      analysis: '"When you durst do it, then you were a man" — Lady Macbeth weaponises Jacobean masculinity itself as a mechanism of manipulation. She does not argue for the murder on moral or practical grounds but attacks Macbeth\'s identity: his manhood is contingent on his willingness to kill. "Durst" (dared) equates courage with violence in a formulation that eliminates all ethical nuance. The conditional "when" implies he was briefly a man and has now regressed — she is emasculating him deliberately in order to provoke action. The dramatic irony accumulates across the play: Macduff, the character who kills Macbeth and restores order, defines manhood in Act 4 as the ability to feel grief ("I must also feel it as a man"). Shakespeare offers Macduff\'s definition as the deliberate correction of Lady Macbeth\'s — the play ultimately argues that authentic masculinity includes conscience and emotion, not the excision of them.',
