      themes: ['ambition', 'fate'],
      devices: ['soliloquy', 'metaphor', 'hamartia'],
      analysis: 'The extended equestrian metaphor is exquisitely controlled: a rider needs a spur to drive his horse forward, but Macbeth identifies that he has no legitimate motivation (no "spur") — no moral justification, no political necessity, no personal grievance. His only reason is ambition. Then the metaphor twists on itself: ambition is itself the vaulting rider, but one who leaps too high and "o\'erleaps itself" — falls on the other side of the horse entirely. The self-destructive logic is already visible to Macbeth before he acts. "O\'erleaps itself" is prophetically precise — he can see that his ambition will cause his downfall but cannot stop it. This is his hamartia stated with perfect clarity: he knows his flaw, names it, and proceeds anyway. The soliloquy reveals not a man deceived but a man who chooses his own destruction.',
      context: 'In classical tragedy, the hamartia (fatal flaw) is the quality that makes a great man great but, taken to excess, brings about his destruction. Shakespeare follows this structure meticulously — Macbeth\'s courage and decisive action are the qualities that made him Scotland\'s greatest soldier, but ambition pushes those same qualities past the boundary of morality. That he identifies his hamartia so lucidly and yet continues makes his tragedy more disturbing than if he were simply ignorant. Aristotle\'s theory of tragedy requires the audience to feel both pity and fear — pity for a man who sees his fate clearly, and fear because his self-knowledge cannot save him.',
