      context: 'The dagger soliloquy raises the central interpretive question about the supernatural in Macbeth: are the witches, the ghost, and the dagger real supernatural phenomena, or are they manifestations of a psyche under extreme pressure? A Jacobean audience, who believed literally in both witchcraft and demonic temptation, would likely have read the dagger as a genuine demonic apparition sent to drive Macbeth to murder. A modern reading tends toward psychology — the hallucination as a product of guilt and ambition. Shakespeare preserves the ambiguity deliberately, ensuring the play speaks to both interpretations.',
