      themes: ['guilt', 'gender', 'deception'],
      devices: ['dramatic_irony', 'structural_irony'],
      analysis: '"A little water" — the modifier "little" is the key word, reducing the murder of a divinely appointed king to a trivial domestic act of washing. She speaks with the brisk efficiency of someone clearing a kitchen surface rather than someone who has just participated in regicide. The line creates immediate dramatic irony: we have just watched Macbeth\'s psychological collapse as he contemplates oceans turning red, and Lady Macbeth dismisses all of it in five syllables. But the structural irony that operates across the full play is the more devastating of the two: by Act 5, Lady Macbeth is sleepwalking in the dark, compulsively scrubbing at a spot of blood that exists only in her imagination, desperately repeating "Out, damned spot." The "little water" that was supposed to clear everything cannot clean a single imaginary mark. Shakespeare stages this as the play\'s most precise structural reversal: the woman who dismissed guilt with confident pragmatism is destroyed by it.',
      context: 'Lady Macbeth\'s line reflects a worldview where guilt is primarily social rather than psychological — she believes that if no one sees the blood, there is no blood. Her philosophy is pragmatic and materialist: the crime exists only in its physical evidence, and physical evidence can be removed. Shakespeare\'s play argues the opposite position with great force: guilt is not external and removable but internal and permanent. Her arc from "a little water" to "all the perfumes of Arabia will not sweeten this little hand" is the play\'s central moral argument enacted in character rather than stated in dialogue.',
